  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a contactless card talk to the reader?",
      "a": "The reader makes a weak radio field. Within a few centimetres, the field powers the card's chip through a tiny wire antenna, and the chip sends back payment details and a one-time code.",
      "asks": [
        "How does contactless work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a card network?",
      "a": "A company, like Visa, Mastercard, UnionPay or RuPay, that passes payment messages between the shop's bank and your bank and sets the rules for card payments.",
      "asks": [
        "What do Visa and Mastercard do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does my bank decide to approve a payment?",
      "a": "Its computers check that the card and code are real, that there is enough money or credit, and that the purchase doesn't look like fraud. This takes under a second.",
      "asks": [
        "Why was my card declined?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Does the money move straight away?",
      "a": "Not quite. The approval sets the money aside, and the banks settle the actual transfer later, usually within a day or two.",
      "asks": [
        "When does the shop get the money?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does contactless need a PIN sometimes?",
      "a": "For safety, larger payments, or several taps in a row, may ask for your PIN to prove it's really you.",
      "asks": [
        "Why did the card machine ask for my PIN?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Contactless payments use near-field communication, which works only over a few centimetres.",
    "RuPay is India's domestic card network, launched by the National Payments Corporation of India."
  ],
